WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.723
- This is the Royal Air Force
training centrifuge at Farnborough.

00:00:03.723 --> 00:00:05.140
And the team here are
going to push me

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.070
as far as they're allowed
to push a civilian.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:10.090
- The centrifuge has been here since 1955.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.103
The device was originally installed
for research purposes,

00:00:13.103 --> 00:00:16.880
although these days it's used for training
as much as it is for research.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.860
What it does is recreate the forces
that you feel in an aircraft.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:24.940
For our routine pilot training, the
first time pilots go on the centrifuge,

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.920
we expect them to get up to 5g
without a g-suit,

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.270
and then up to 7g
with an anti-g-suit.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.180
- Now I'm not taking a
significant risk here.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.180
I'm healthy, I've pulled a few gs before.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.250
And the human body can take this.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.580
And the reason we know that
is because in the 1950s,

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:45.750
the US Air Force used rocket sleds
to push volunteers to incredible speeds.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:49.140
But that rocket-powered acceleration
wasn't the dangerous,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.660
or even the really
high-g part of the test.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.890
See, high-g acceleration takes a lot
of incredibly expensive rockets

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.800
or a big ol' centrifuge like this.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.507
But high-g deceleration?

00:01:00.507 --> 00:01:02.600
All you need for that is a wall.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.896
Or for something less destructive,
like the rocket sleds,

00:01:04.896 --> 00:01:07.540
you use scoops
dropped down into a water trough.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.800
USAF flight surgeon John Stapp,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.520
aboard the rocket sled
Sonic Wind Number One,

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.720
holds the record for the
highest sustained g-force

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.680
anyone has ever voluntarily endured,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.897
25g for 1.1 seconds,
with a brief peak over 46g.

00:01:20.897 --> 00:01:23.370
And he was badly injured,
but he survived

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.730
and he recovered and he
lived to the age of 89.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:28.550
The human body is an incredible thing

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:31.890
particularly because
we didn't evolve for this.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.460
- G-tolerance is something
that's innate in all of us.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.740
Some of us have high G-tolerance.
Some of us have low G-tolerance.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.180
Over the years, people's g-tolerance
doesn't really adapt.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.840
Any shortfall in g-tolerance
has really got to be made up

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.570
by physical exertion
and the g-straining manoeuvre.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:49.440
- What this centrifuge doesn't have
is much jerk.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.771
And jerk is a technical term.

00:01:50.771 --> 00:01:54.880
In the same way that acceleration
is the rate of change of speed,

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.130
jerk is the rate of change
of acceleration.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.630
And because it takes time to
spin up and spin down...

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.590
Oh, here we go! Even though
the acceleration is high,

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:07.130
the jerk here is relatively
low, about 1g per second.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.140
Jerk is the difference
between a rocket to space

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:12.680
which might take a couple of minutes
to reach peak acceleration

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.520
and a fighter jet, where manoeuvres might
change the G force acting on you

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:17.570
in a fraction of a second.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.230
And it can go further than that.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:24.150
You can measure the rate of change of jerk
which is called snap or jounce.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.560
The two derivatives after that are called
crackle and pop but

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.920
they're not all that useful
in the real world.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.170
- As we increase the G
that Tom is exposed to,

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.950
the blood's going to be
pushed down into his feet,

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.820
and he's going to have to work really hard
to push that blood back up

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.610
to feed oxygen to his brain
to keep him conscious.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:46.920
And in real life, we would be expecting
that person to be flying an aircraft whilst doing that.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:49.480
- I'm getting a little bit of grey-out.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.680
I can't quite see. Agh.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.960
- We teach the anti-g straining manoeuvre
which composes of two separate parts.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.080
First of all, muscle tensing,

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.290
so both the buttocks and legs
squeezing the blood vessels

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.063
to try and get the blood
back up into the chest and the head.

00:03:05.063 --> 00:03:07.730
But also the second part
is a breathing manoeuvre

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.820
which increases the strain in the chest

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:15.450
directly increasing the blood pressure
to the great vessels in the chest

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.870
and keeping him conscious.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:19.250
And when you lose
blood pressure to your head,

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:20.980
you could even lose consciousness.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.910
And we term that g-induced
loss of consciousness or G-LOC.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.680
[gasping]

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.580
- Blimey!

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.600
I lost everything there.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.200
Wow.

00:03:48.203 --> 00:03:50.400
- G-LOC in itself is not dangerous.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.890
But the real point is when you G-LOC
you're flying an aircraft.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:56.100
So if you're not able
to fly that aircraft,

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.070
I'm sure you can appreciate
that that is a real problem.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.280
- Because of John Stapp
and all the volunteers like him

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:02.980
that rode the rocket sleds,

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.400
there is a lot of research

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.960
into acceleration on the human body.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.910
How many gs can be withstood
for minutes at a time?

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.300
How many gs can be
withstood for brief moments?

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.010
And how many can be withstood with
training that I clearly don't have.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.870
Rocket scientists and
roller coaster designers use that data.

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:22.760
But there's not much research into jerk

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:27.540
because how do you test it without
also testing acceleration?

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.060
Over on the Starrship channel,
I am not passing out

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.260
pulling gs with the
Blades aerobatic team(!)

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.210
And as for this video, thank you so much

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.720
to all the team at the
RAF Centre of Aviation Medicine,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.463
to the team at Qinetiq, and
to the team at Starrship.

